tering the path to your model the index of your USB camera and the resolution you want to run at for example you can use python YOLO detect. pi-- model equals YOLO 11n ncnn model Source equals USB 0 re", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=z70ZrSZNi-8\u0026t=449s", "end": 557.63, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "90faed61bd1a0edde76109530cd63f2014bf31ac087ef0b390fa45b4ef74bd6a", "start": 449.51, "text": "help this script can be used as a start starting point for other cool applications the Raspberry Pi's gpio can be used to control a wide range of Hardware devices and Trigger actions when certain events occur as an example I created a smart", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}], "current_topic": "Uncategorized", "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpts": [{"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=z70ZrSZNi-8\u0026t=15s", "end": 132.19, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "a5a8a6150b41b50381886b5b6d7ebfb83470d9f38f0234942d0d90db6fc3a521", "start": 15.509, "text": "[Music] hey everyone in this video we'll walk through the process of setting up the Raspberry Pi to run YOLO object detection models the Raspberry Pi is just powerful enough to run lightweight YOLO models in real time making it great for co", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=z70ZrSZNi-8\u0026t=132s", "end": 226.19, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "b44bde05bfda4b22aa0479d09bf9a27fd76348527662f12e875f312b2de14c06", "start": 132.2, "text": "activate when the environment is active VM will appear in front of the command prompt if you ever close and reopen the terminal window then you'll need to reactivate the environment by moving into the YOLO folder and using Source vmb activa", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=z70ZrSZNi-8\u0026t=226s", "end": 345.59, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "faec2a07e9c375774665ea36c69e0f91ee80b8b3cb7be535b21966b8aaf1f17c", "start": 226.2, "text": "PT you can also use YOLO 11s or YOLO 11 L here but they don't run as fast on the pi see my YOLO comparison video to get an idea of the detection frame rate you can get with each model when the model's finished downloading you'll have a YOLO", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=z70ZrSZNi-8\u0026t=345s", "end": 447.189, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "ebd1fbd7635537ea0442b9820c28132032c8d0cf176afc21e9b593f19573d18c", "start": 345.6, "text": "ed. codolo detect. run the script by entering the path to your model the index of your USB camera and the resolution you want to run at for example you can use python YOLO detect. pi-- model equals YOLO 11n ncnn model Source equals USB 0 re", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=z70ZrSZNi-8\u0026t=449s", "end": 557.63, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "90faed61bd1a0edde76109530cd63f2014bf31ac087ef0b390fa45b4ef74bd6a", "start": 449.51, "text": "help this script can be used as a start starting point for other cool applications the Raspberry Pi's gpio can be used to control a wide range of Hardware devices and Trigger actions when certain events occur as an example I created a smart", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}], "hint": {"kind": "opening_prose", "text": "\u003c!-- HOOK_START --\u003e \u003c!-- HOOK_END --\u003e The Raspberry Pi is just powerful enough to run lightweight YOLO11 object detection models in real-time. This makes it ideal for edge AI applications that act on visual data from the world around them. In this video, we'll walk through the process of setting up Ultralytics YOLO11 models to run on the Raspberry Pi with a custom Python script. The video also gives an example of a \"smart lamp\" program that shows how to use Raspberry Pi GPIO to turn on a lamp when a person is detected sitting on the couch. A written version of this tutorial is available on my ", "truncated": true}, "platform": "youtube", "profile": "librarian", "schema_version": 1, "selection_version": "spread-longest-v1", "slug": "How_to_Run_YOLO_Object_Detection_Models_on_the_Raspberry_Pi", "source_revision": "ccdde01a0e23e3d3b4c69615c3ba67ebdddf0af9c6726fc5ea6956f80b822c63", "source_type": "video", "status": "evidenced", "summary_hint": "\u003c!-- HOOK_START --\u003e \u003c!-- HOOK_END --\u003e The Raspberry Pi is just powerful enough to run lightweight YOLO11 object detection models in real-time. This makes it ideal for edge AI applications that act on visual data from the world around them. In this video, we'll walk through the process of setting up Ultralytics YOLO11 models to run on the Raspberry Pi with a custom Python script. The video also gives an example of a \"smart lamp\" program that shows how to use Raspberry Pi GPIO to turn on a lamp when a person is detected sitting on the couch. A written version of this tutorial is available on my ", "title": "How to Run YOLO Object Detection Models on the Raspberry Pi", "topic": "Uncategorized", "truncated": true, "truncation": {"byte_budget": false, "fields": [], "selection": false}, "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z70ZrSZNi-8", "video_id": "z70ZrSZNi-8", "yoinked_at": "2026-07-09T19:23:09"}
